course: biology
lesson: 192
title: Dogs: from wolves to hundreds of breeds
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the park a tiny chihuahua and a huge Great Dane sniff each other hello. One could almost stand under the other, yet they greet each other as dogs, because they are one species.
explain: All dogs come from wolves that started living near people more than fifteen thousand years ago; much later people chose which dogs had puppies, keeping the ones best at a job or with a look they liked.
explain: That is how we got sheepdogs that herd, terriers that dig after rats, retrievers that bring back birds from water, hounds that follow a scent and greyhounds built to sprint.
explain: Breeding changed bodies a lot: a dachshund's short legs were made for badger burrows, while a pug's flat face, chosen for looks, can make breathing hard.
explain: Inside every breed the wolf still shows: a family dog may tilt its head or howl along when it hears a wolf on television.
fact: There are around four hundred dog breeds in the world, and mixed-breed dogs are often very healthy.
fact: A chihuahua and a Great Dane are the same species and could in theory have puppies together.
fact: Dogs still carry wolf behaviours such as howling, digging and guarding food, and some howl along with sirens because the long wail sounds a bit like a howl.
fact: Sheepdogs herd, terriers dig after rats, retrievers carry back shot birds gently in soft mouths, and hounds follow scents or chase by sight.
fact: Dachshund means badger dog in German; its long body and short legs let it follow badgers underground, but its long back is easily hurt by jumping off sofas.
fact: Pugs and bulldogs were bred for flatter and flatter faces, which squeeze their airways so they snore and can struggle to breathe; vets now advise choosing dogs with slightly longer noses.
fact: Greyhounds can reach about seventy kilometres an hour thanks to long legs, a deep chest with big lungs and a bendy spine, yet at home they sleep most of the day.
fact: Labradors were bred to fetch from water, with a thick waterproof coat and a thick otter-like tail used as a rudder.
fact: Newborn puppies cannot see or hear for about the first two weeks and rely on smell, touch and their mother's warmth; at about eight weeks they are ready for a new home.
fact: The rule of seven dog years for each human year is only a rough guess: dogs grow up very fast in the first year, then age more slowly, and small dogs usually live longer than big ones.
fact: A one-year-old dog is more like a teenager than a seven-year-old child.
fact: Dogs cool down mostly by panting, because they sweat only a little, through the pads of their paws, so a dog left in a hot car can overheat in minutes, even with a window open.
fact: When a dog and its owner look at each other, both get a rush of a bonding chemical in the brain.
exercise: Why do a chihuahua and a Great Dane greet each other as dogs? | They are the same species | They are cousins from different species | They were both bred from foxes | They smell exactly alike
exercise: About how many dog breeds are there in the world? | Around four hundred | About ten | About forty thousand | Exactly one hundred
exercise: How did so many different breeds appear? | People chose which dogs had puppies, for jobs and looks | Wolves split into many breeds on their own in the wild, long before people | Each breed came from a different wild animal | Dogs changed shape to match their owners
exercise: Why might a family dog tilt its head or howl when a wolf howls on TV? | It hears a sound its own kind once used to call the pack | It thinks the TV is broken and is calling for someone to fix it | Dogs hate all music | It is asking for its dinner, because howling always means hunger
exercise: Which job was a terrier bred for? | Digging after rats and other small animals | Herding sheep and cattle on hills and farms | Pulling sleds | Fetching shot birds out of lakes and rivers
exercise: What does a retriever do on a hunt? | Carries back the birds | Herds the sheep back home to the farm | Digs badgers out of their setts | Guards the house at night
exercise: Hounds were bred mainly to hunt how? | By following a scent or chasing by sight | By digging deep tunnels after rabbits and moles | By swimming under water | By climbing trees and waiting on the branches
exercise: What does the German name dachshund mean? | Badger dog | Sausage dog | Short dog | Running dog
exercise: Why should a dachshund use a ramp instead of jumping off the sofa? | Its long back is easily hurt | Its short legs would grow longer | Its long floppy ears would get tangled | It cannot see the floor from up high
exercise: Why does a pug snore and breathe noisily? | Its short flat face squeezes its airways | It always has a cold | Its tongue is too big for any dog | It is trying to talk
exercise: What helps a greyhound run so fast? | Long legs, a deep chest with big lungs and a bendy spine | Short legs and a heavy, solid body that keeps it steady on corners | Webbed feet | A long fluffy tail that it spins round like a propeller
exercise: How do greyhounds usually spend the day at home? | Sleeping most of it | Running round the garden non-stop | Digging holes in the flower beds | Swimming in the nearest pond
exercise: What was the labrador's tail useful for in the water? | Steering like a rudder | Keeping its head dry above the water | Splashing fish towards the bank | Holding on to sticks floating past
exercise: What can a newborn puppy do in its first two weeks? | Smell and feel, but not see or hear | See and hear perfectly, just like its mother does | Run and bark | Eat dog biscuits and drink from a bowl on its own
exercise: Is a dog's age really seven dog years for each human year? | No, dogs grow up fast in the first year and then age more slowly | Yes, exactly seven dog years for every year, for every kind of dog | No, it is ten for every dog | Yes, but only for big dogs, which grow up the slowest
exercise: Which dogs usually live longer? | Small dogs | Big dogs with strong hearts | Dogs with long ears | Dogs born in winter
exercise: Why is a hot car so dangerous for a dog? | The dog cannot pant out the heat fast enough and overheats in minutes | Dogs are scared of cars, and the fear alone makes their poor hearts beat far too fast | The seats are uncomfortable | The window glass magnifies the sun and burns their eyes and fur
test: «my nan's dachshund looks like a sausage on legs, why'd anyone make a dog like that??» What is the real reason? | Hunters wanted a dog that could follow badgers into their burrows | It was made for sitting on laps and fitting under small chairs | Short legs stop it running away from home | It is a cross between a dog and a ferret, which is why it is so long
test: Count them: at the dog show a sheepdog, a terrier, a retriever and a hound line up. How many legs and how many tails? | 16 legs and 4 tails | 12 legs and 4 tails | 16 legs and 8 tails | 20 legs and 4 tails
test: «we want a puppy that can breathe properly and go on long walks, the vet said pick one with a longer nose. why??» What is right? | Very flat faces squeeze the airways, so longer noses breathe and run more easily | Longer noses can smell the path much better, so the dog never gets lost on long walks | Flat-faced dogs never need walks | The vet just likes long noses
test: «our dog Max is 2, so he's like 14 in people years, a kid» What is closer to the truth? | Max is already a young grown-up; dogs grow up very fast in the first year | Yes, he is exactly 14, because every single dog year is worth seven of our years | No, he is still a baby | No, he is 2 in people years too, just like a child of two
test: Count them: a mother dog has 6 puppies, all with their eyes still shut. How many closed eyes are there? | 12 | 6 | 14 | 24
test: What would happen if a labrador with a thick waterproof coat and a greyhound with a thin coat both swam in a cold lake? | The labrador would stay much warmer | The greyhound would stay warmer | Both would feel the same | The labrador would sink
test: In a cartoon a dog sweats buckets after chasing the postman. What do real dogs do? | They pant, because they sweat only through their paw pads | They sweat all over their bodies through their fur, just like people do | They sweat only from their ears | They cannot cool down at all
test: «mixed breed dogs are always sicker than posh pedigree ones» What is right? | No, mixed-breed dogs are often very healthy | Yes, every mixed dog is ill, because its parents did not match | Yes, only pedigrees live long | No, mixed dogs never get ill
test: «greyhound in a flat? are you mad, it'll need running all day» What is the surprise? | Greyhounds are sprinters and sleep most of the day at home | Yes, it must run for hours every day or it will become ill and sad | Greyhounds never sleep | Greyhounds can only live on racetracks with other racing dogs
test: Your dog stares at you, you stare back and smile. What happens inside both of you? | Both get a rush of a bonding chemical | The dog feels angry at being stared at | Only you feel anything, the dog feels nothing | The dog gets sleepy and nothing else happens
test: Why did people breed retrievers with soft mouths and a love of water, rather than breeding them for speed? | Their job was to bring shot birds back from water without damaging them | Speed was against the law for hunting dogs in those days, so breeders had to choose something else | Water dogs needed no food, which saved their owners money | Soft mouths made them bark less
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the chihuahua and the Great Dane? | They are members of a single species, differing by reason of selective breeding by man | They are members of distinct species descended from distinct wild ancestors | They are members of a single breed differing only in age | They are members of the same genus but cannot interbreed in principle
exam: Identify the purpose for which the dachshund was originally bred. | The pursuit of badgers within their burrows | The herding of sheep upon open hillsides | The retrieval of waterfowl from lakes | The pulling of sleds across snow
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of laboured breathing in the pug and the bulldog. | The flattening of the face through breeding, which constricts the airways | An inherited fondness for snoring, passed down unchanged from the wolf | The excessive length of the tongue, which blocks the throat during sleep | The habitual consumption of cold water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rule that one year of a dog's life equals seven human years. | It is a rough approximation, dogs maturing rapidly in the first year and ageing more slowly thereafter | It is an exact law of nature applying equally to all breeds, whatever their size or their weight | It applies exclusively to the largest breeds, the smaller ones ageing at the same rate as men | It has been proved correct for all mammals
